    <h2>Should You Play In 2026?</h2>

    <p>
        Absolutely, especially if your sense of humour is compatible with animated historical portraits, complete visual nonsense and a game that is absolutely not afraid to get its weird on. Rock of Ages 3 has a crazy sense of style, sticks to it completely and regularly had me sitting there going, “OMG, what am I actually looking at?”
    </p>

    <p>
        Underneath all that madness is a game that is easy to pick up but surprisingly difficult to master. The boulder levels are absolute physics chaos. Controlling a massive rock sounds straightforward enough, but getting it through each level successfully can quickly become a test of timing, control and your ability to recover when the physics inevitably decide they have other plans.
    </p>

    <p>
        The Tower Defence-style levels demand a completely different kind of thinking. These were genuinely on another level for me, requiring much more planning and tactical awareness than the boulder stages. The contrast between the two styles gives the game variety, but it also means being comfortable with one side of the experience will not necessarily help you with the other.
    </p>

    <p>
        The difficulty generally felt fair. There is absolutely a challenge here for anyone looking for one, particularly in the Tower Defence levels, but it never reached the point where the game felt unplayable. It usually felt like there was something I could do differently, even when actually doing it was another matter entirely.
    </p>

    <p>
        I did eventually run into a bit of a progression wall. Once I had cleaned out the levels I was actually good at, moving forward became more of a struggle because I had to confront the parts of the game that did not play to my strengths. I would put at least some of that down to me rather than the game, but it could still be off-putting for players who strongly prefer one level type over another.
    </p>

    <p>
        The humour was absolutely my particular brand of crack. Rock of Ages 3 does not occasionally make a weird joke and then return to normal. The weirdness is the normal, and the game commits to it so confidently that I could not help but love it.
    </p>

    <p>
        I particularly enjoyed the presentation. The use of actual historical portraits brought to life through deliberately ridiculous animation is hilarious, while the music is genuinely epic. The rock remixes of classical pieces fit the chaos perfectly and help make the entire experience feel even more dramatic than a game about rolling giant boulders has any right to be.
    </p>

    <p>
        That style will not work for everyone. The humour is extremely goofy and could become grating if you do not click with it. Rock of Ages 3 is so committed to its identity that your enjoyment will depend heavily on whether you are willing to meet it on its own wonderfully strange wavelength.
    </p>

    <p>
        The gameplay could also become a little repetitive after a while. Combined with the difficulty I had progressing once I had exhausted the levels that suited me, the main loop started to lose some of its freshness. Neither issue ruined the experience, but together they stopped it from reaching a higher score for me.
    </p>

    <p>
        Even with those frustrations, I absolutely loved my time with Rock of Ages 3. It is funny, challenging and completely confident in what it wants to be. If chaotic physics, tactical defence and animated historical nonsense sound like your idea of a good time, this is absolutely worth playing.
    </p>

